The '''2026 Lambeth London Borough Council election''' took place on 7 May 2026, as part of the [[2026 United Kingdom local elections]]. All 63 members of [[Lambeth London Borough Council]] were elected. The election took place alongside [[2026 London local elections|local elections in the other London boroughs]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Coughlan |first=Joe |date=2025-04-30 |title=London local elections, why they aren't in 2025 and when the next ones will be |url=https://www.mylondon.news/news/uk-world-news/london-local-elections-arent-any-31429137 |access-date=2025-09-05 |website=My London |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Local authority, combined authority, and county combined authority election cycles in England |url=https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/election-timetable-in-england/election-timetable-in-england |access-date=2025-09-05 |website=GOV.UK |language=en}}</ref>

==Background==
=== History ===
[[File:Lambeth London UK local election 2022 map.svg|thumb|Result of the 2022 election]]
The thirty-two [[London boroughs]] were established in 1965 by the [[London Government Act 1963]]. They are the principal authorities in Greater London and have responsibilities including education, housing, planning, highways, social services, libraries, recreation, waste, environmental health and revenue collection. Some of the powers are shared with the [[Greater London Authority]], which also manages passenger transport, police and fire.<ref>{{cite web|title=The essential guide to London local government {{!}} London Councils|url=https://www.londoncouncils.gov.uk/who-runs-london/essential-guide-london-local-government|access-date=10 October 2021|website=www.londoncouncils.gov.uk}}</ref>

Since its formation, [[Lambeth London Borough Council|Lambeth]] has generally been under [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] control apart from one period from 1968 to 1971 of [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative]] control and several periods of [[no overall control]]. The council was controlled by a [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrat]]-[[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative]] [[Coalition government|coalition]] from 2002 to 2006, and since 2006 has continuously had a [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] majority.

==Changes since 2022==
===By-elections===
Following the 2022 elections, the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]] secured a majority on Lambeth Council with 58 seats. The [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrats]] formed the official opposition with three seats, while the [[Green Party of England and Wales|Green Party]] won two.

A by-election in the Vauxhall ward was held in October 2023, following the death of Labour councillor Liam Jarnecki. Labour retained the seat, with Tom Swaine-Jameson.<ref name="auto">{{Cite web|url=https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=2022_Lambeth_London_Borough_Council_election&oldid=1353324803|title=2022 Lambeth London Borough Council election|date=9 May 2026|accessdate=9 May 2026|via=Wikipedia}}</ref>

Two by-elections took place in May 2024. In Knight’s Hill, Labour councillor Sonia Winifred resigned after having the [[party whip]] suspended for defying it in a Gaza ceasefire vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.standard.co.uk/news/politics/labour-party-lambeth-council-whip-gaza-ceasefire-vote-b1141958.html|title=Anger as councillors have Labour whip suspended after voting for Gaza ceasefire|first=Lydia|last=Chantler-Hicks|date=28 February 2024|website=The Standard|accessdate=9 May 2026}}</ref> Labour held the seat, with Emma Nye elected. On the same day, a by-election was held in Streatham Common and Vale following the resignation of [[Tom Rutland]], who stepped down to focus on contesting [[East Worthing and Shoreham]]. Labour retained the seat, electing Sarah Cole.

A further by-election in Streatham Common and Vale took place in July 2024 after the resignation of Hena Shah. The seat was again held by Labour, with Dominic Armstrong elected.<ref name="auto"/><ref name="auto"/>

In May 2025, a by-election was held in Herne Hill and Loughborough Junction following the election of [[Jim Dickson (politician)|Jim Dickson]] as MP for [[Dartford (UK Parliament constituency)|Dartford]]. The seat was gained by the Green Party, with Paul Valentine elected, making the Greens the joint opposition party on the council.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.brixtonbuzz.com/2025/05/greens-edge-out-labour-in-herne-hill-and-loughborough-junction-upset-as-valentine-claims-council-seat/|title=Greens edge out Labour in Herne Hill and Loughborough Junction upset as Valentine claims Council seat|first=Jason|last=Cobb|date=2 May 2025|accessdate=9 May 2026}}</ref>

===Affiliation changes===
Cllr Martin Abrams resigned from the Labour Party in August 2025, highlighting a culture of alleged bullying and factionalism. He was suspended by Lambeth Labour in early 2024 after voting for a ceasefire motion in Gaza.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://insidecroydon.com/2025/08/29/jewish-councillor-quits-labour-over-bullying-and-anti-semitism/|title=Jewish councillor quits Labour claiming he has been bullied|date=29 August 2025|website=Inside Croydon|accessdate=9 May 2026}}</ref> He joined the Greens in September 2025, making them the official opposition group on Lambeth Council.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.brixtonbuzz.com/2025/09/ex-labour-councillor-martin-abrams-defects-to-lambeth-greens-party-now-becomes-official-opposition-at-town-hall/|title=Ex-Labour councillor Martin Abrams defects to Lambeth Greens – party now becomes official opposition at Town Hall|first=Jason|last=Cobb|date=11 September 2025|accessdate=9 May 2026}}</ref>

In July 2025, Cllr Irfan Mohammed was suspended from Labour after being charged with a criminal offences relating to exposure, controlling behaviour and sexual assault.<ref>{{cite web | title=UPDATE: Full details emerge after Lambeth Labour councillor Irfan Mohammed charged with controlling behaviour, sexual assault and exposure | url=https://www.brixtonbuzz.com/2025/07/update-full-details-emerge-after-lambeth-labour-councillor-irfan-mohammed-charged-with-controlling-behaviour-sexual-assault-and-exposure/ }}</ref> He sat as an Independent.<ref>{{cite web | title=Lambeth Labour councillor charged and suspended as party moves to limit fallout | url=https://www.brixtonbuzz.com/2025/07/lambeth-labour-councillor-charged-and-suspended-as-party-moves-to-limit-fallout/ }}</ref>

In November 2025, Cllr Christiana (Tina) Valcarcel, defected to the [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrats]], making them the joint opposition party on the council.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Burford |first=Rachael |date=2025-11-12 |title=London Labour sees more defections as support for Keir Starmer’s party plunges |url=https://www.standard.co.uk/news/politics/london-labour-defections-councillors-keir-starmer-lambeth-b1257406.html |access-date=2025-11-20 |website=The Standard |language=en}}</ref>

